.TH ERASYNC 1 2026-11-12 erasync "User Commands"
.SH NAME
erasync \- copy files and trees, using dm-era to find what changed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B erasync
.RI [ options ]
.I SRC DEST
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B erasync
makes
.I DEST
a copy of the directory tree
.IR SRC .
.I SRC
may also be a single file, symlink or special file: it is written to
.IR DEST ,
or into
.I DEST
when
.I DEST
is a directory.
.PP
Tools such as
.BR rsync (1)
read and hash whole files on both sides to find what changed.
For very large files with small changes, such as virtual machine images
and databases, that costs far more than the copy itself.
.B erasync
asks dm-era instead.
A regular file on a dm-era device is updated from the blocks written
since the era recorded on the destination, plus any blocks written on
the destination itself when it is also on dm-era.
The first copy of a file is the whole file.
Later copies read and write only the blocks that changed.
.PP
Both
.I SRC
and
.I DEST
must be on dm-era devices.
.B erasync
refuses to copy anything else; use
.BR cp (1)
or
.BR rsync (1)
for that.
A regular file below
.I SRC
that is on another filesystem, which is not on dm-era, is reported as an
error and skipped.
.PP
Regular files, directories, symlinks, fifos, sockets and device nodes
are replicated.
Symlinks are copied as symlinks and are not followed.
Device nodes are recreated.
A hard link is copied as a file of its own.
Mode, owner and mtime are copied.
Entries in
.I DEST
that are not in
.I SRC
are left in place unless
.B \-\-delete
is given.
.PP
A whole file is written to a temporary file and renamed into place, so
the destination is never half written.
Names in
.I DEST
that start with
.B .erasync.tmp.\&
are unfinished copies and are removed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be done, without writing anything or taking era
snapshots.
Files are compared by size and mtime only.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Also show unchanged files, and how each file was copied: the bytes sent,
the bytes the copy had to read and the size of the file.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Show errors only, and no copy progress.
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Remove entries in
.I DEST
that are not in
.IR SRC .
This applies to a directory tree, and the walk does not follow symlinks.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " PATTERN"
Skip entries below
.I SRC
that match
.IR PATTERN ,
and everything below an excluded directory.
With
.BR \-\-delete ,
entries in
.I DEST
that match are kept.
May be given more than once; an entry is skipped when any pattern
matches.
See
.B PATTERNS
below.
.TP
.BR \-\-force [ =\fIPATTERN\fR ]
Copy files whole instead of only the blocks dm-era saw change, and store
a fresh era record.
Without a pattern this applies to every regular file, for a full resync.
With a pattern it applies to the files that match, and to everything
below a directory that matches.
The pattern needs the
.BR = :
.B \-\-force
followed by a separate word means a plain
.BR \-\-force .
May be given more than once.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-rsh " \fICMD\fR"
The remote shell to run instead of
.BR ssh .
.I CMD
is split at spaces.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote\-path " PROG"
The
.B erasync
to run on the remote host.
The default is
.B erasync
on the remote
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B \-\-server
Speak the copy protocol on standard input and output.
This is what the remote side runs; it is not meant to be used directly.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the usage.
.SH PATTERNS
The patterns of
.B \-\-exclude
and
.B \-\-force
are shell wildcards, matched with
.BR fnmatch (3).
.IP \(bu 2
A pattern without a
.B /
matches the name of an entry at any depth:
.B *\-tester*.qcow2
skips every file whose name matches, in every directory.
.IP \(bu 2
A pattern with a
.B /
matches the end of the path of an entry below
.IR SRC ,
and
.B *
does not match a
.BR / :
.B vm/*.img
matches
.I vm/a.img
and
.IR old/vm/b.img .
A leading
.B /
anchors the pattern at the top of
.IR SRC :
.B /logs
matches
.I SRC/logs
only.
.IP \(bu 2
A trailing
.B /
makes the pattern match directories only:
.B cache/
skips every directory named cache.
.PP
Quote the pattern, or escape its wildcards, so that the shell does not
expand it:
.B \-\-exclude='*\-tester*.qcow2'
or
.BR \-\-exclude=\e*\-tester\e*.qcow2 .
.SH REMOTE COPIES
Either
.I SRC
or
.I DEST
may be
.RI [ USER @] HOST : PATH ,
or
.RI [ HOST ]: PATH
for an IPv6 address.
.B erasync
runs
.B erasync \-\-server
on that host over
.BR ssh ,
and the two copies of the program talk over that connection.
One side must be local.
A path with a slash before the first colon is a local name.
.PP
The era of a device can only be read on the host that owns the device,
so each side reads its own dm-era device and era log.
Mounting a remote
.I DEST
over NFS and copying to it locally does not see the destination era.
.PP
Both hosts must run versions of
.B erasync
that speak the same protocol.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B erasync
must run as root, on both hosts of a remote copy, because the dm-era
metadata and the era logs are only readable by root.
It needs
.BR era_invalidate (8),
.BR fnmatch (3)
from thin-provisioning-tools, and user extended attributes on the
destination filesystem.
.PP
A dm-era device allows one metadata snapshot at a time.
Runs that use the same device wait for each other, for up to ten
minutes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B user.erasync.era
An extended attribute on each destination file.
It records the source era the bytes came from and, once the copy is
complete, the destination era after which a write means the copy was
changed by something other than
.BR erasync .
A file without a valid record is copied whole.
.TP
.BI /var/lib/erasync/era/ device-id
The log of eras for one dm-era device.
An era number is only trusted when its exact entry is in this log,
because recreating the dm-era metadata restarts the numbering.
Deleting a log makes the next run copy the files on that device whole
once.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything was copied.
.TP
.B 1
Some entries could not be copied; the errors were printed.
.TP
.B 2
The copy could not be run or was interrupted, for example because of a
bad argument, a missing requirement, a broken connection or a signal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Copy a tree of images, then keep it up to date:
.PP
.RS
.nf
erasync \-v /vm/images /backup/images
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy one image:
.PP
.RS
.nf
erasync /vm/images/disk.qcow2 /backup/images/disk.qcow2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy a tree of images, without the scratch images of test machines:
.PP
.RS
.nf
erasync \-\-exclude='*\-tester*.qcow2' /vm/images /backup/images
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy the base images whole, and the rest as usual:
.PP
.RS
.nf
erasync \-\-force='*\-base*.qcow2' /vm/images /backup/images
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy a tree to another host, and remove what is gone from the source:
.PP
.RS
.nf
erasync \-\-delete /vm/images backup.example.com:/backup/images
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
A file that is being written while it is copied, such as the disk of a
running virtual machine, may be copied in an inconsistent state.
dm-era sees those writes, and the next run copies them again.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR rsync (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR dmsetup (8),
.BR era_invalidate (8),
.BR fnmatch (3)
